CPU direct convolution (CONV_2D / CONV_3D, the CPU VAE path with --conv-direct auto): build each patch row from a source
row segment converted once. For a run of consecutive patches of one output row, the (input plane, kernel row) source
segment the run reads is converted to the kernel type once (ggml_cpu_fp32_to_fp16 for F16 kernels), then every patch
copies its knl_w taps from it. Before, every source element went through a bounds check and an F32 -> F16 conversion
once per kernel column tap (3 times for a 3x3 kernel) and each store wrote one element. The patch matrix holds the same
values in the same slots, so the GEMM and the output are bit-identical.
The per-tap loop is kept for 1-wide kernels (no element is converted twice there), for segments over 1024 elements
(large strides / dilations) and as the reference path (use_ref), which test-backend-ops -b CPU compares against.

